Step 1: Lemma (sliding for a fixed length). Fix an integer L ≥ 2 and suppose for a sequence (x_n) that T_n = {x_n, ..., x_{n+L-1}} is a set of L consecutive integers for every n. Then for every n:
(i) x_n ∈ {min T_n, max T_n};
(ii) T_{n+1} = T_n ± 1 as sets (i.e., min T_{n+1} = min T_n ± 1 and max T_{n+1} = max T_n ± 1);
(iii) x_{n+L} = x_n ± L.
Proof. Let T_n = {m, m+1, ..., m+L-1}. If x_n were not an endpoint of T_n, then T_n \ {x_n} would have a “hole” in the interior; adding a single integer cannot yield L consecutive integers again. Hence x_n is an endpoint. If x_n = m, then to make T_{n+1} consecutive one must add m+L, giving T_{n+1} = {m+1, ..., m+L}; thus x_{n+L} = m+L = x_n + L. If x_n = m+L-1, symmetrically one must add m-1, yielding T_{n+1} = {m-1, ..., m+L-2} and x_{n+L} = m-1 = x_n - L. This proves (i)–(iii).

Step 4: We determine which factor is divisible by 3. Because 5 ≡ -1 (mod 3),
- if k is even, then 5^k ≡ 1 (mod 3), so 5^k - 1 ≡ 0 (mod 3) and 5^k + 1 ≡ 2 (mod 3);
- if k is odd, then 5^k ≡ -1 (mod 3), so 5^k + 1 ≡ 0 (mod 3) and 5^k - 1 ≡ 1 (mod 3).

Step 7: Conclusion. The largest number of swaps Elijah might need is
- n − 1 if n is odd;
- n/2 if n is even.

