Step 2: Because ord_p(a) | (p − 1), and ord_p(a) ≥ p − 4, the only possibilities are ord_p(a) ∈ {p − 4, p − 3, p − 2, p − 1}. But:
- p − 2 ∤ p − 1, impossible;
- p − 3 | p − 1 implies p − 3 | 2, so p ∈ {4, 5}, impossible for p > 5;
- p − 4 | p − 1 implies p − 4 | 3, so p ∈ {5, 7}. Thus for p > 7 we must have ord_p(a) = p − 1, and for p = 7 it is possible that ord_7(a) = 3.

Step 5: We now solve the condition “{0, 1, a, a^2} is an arithmetic progression in 𝔽_p.” Let the progression be {t, t + d, t + 2d, t + 3d} with d ≠ 0 (mod p). Since 0 belongs to it, for some m ∈ {0, 1, 2, 3} we have t + md = 0, hence {1, a, a^2} = {(1 − m)d, (2 − m)d, (3 − m)d} (as a set). Up to replacing d by −d, this yields two possibilities:
- (A) {1, a, a^2} = {d, 2d, 3d};
- (B) {1, a, a^2} = {−d, d, 2d}.
We treat (A) and (B) separately, by eliminating d.

Case (A): There is a permutation (k_1, k_2, k_3) of (1, 2, 3) with
1 = k_1 d, a = k_2 d, a^2 = k_3 d.
Eliminating d gives (k_2/k_1)^2 = k_3/k_1, i.e. k_2^2 ≡ k_1 k_3 (mod p). Checking all six permutations:
(1, 2, 3): 4 ≡ 3 ⇒ p | 1, impossible;
(1, 3, 2): 9 ≡ 2 ⇒ p = 7;
(2, 1, 3): 1 ≡ 6 ⇒ p = 5;
(2, 3, 1): 9 ≡ 2 ⇒ p = 7;
(3, 1, 2): 1 ≡ 6 ⇒ p = 5;
(3, 2, 1): 4 ≡ 3 ⇒ p | 1, impossible.
Thus the only prime ≥ 5 arising here is p = 7.

Case (B): There is a permutation (k_1, k_2, k_3) of (−1, 1, 2) with
1 = k_1 d, a = k_2 d, a^2 = k_3 d.
Again (k_2/k_1)^2 = k_3/k_1 ⇒ k_2^2 ≡ k_1 k_3 (mod p). Checking all six permutations:
(−1, 1, 2): 1 ≡ −2 ⇒ p = 3;
(−1, 2, 1): 4 ≡ −1 ⇒ p = 5;
(1, −1, 2): 1 ≡ 2 ⇒ p | 1, impossible;
(1, 2, −1): 4 ≡ −1 ⇒ p = 5;
(2, −1, 1): 1 ≡ 2 ⇒ p | 1, impossible;
(2, 1, −1): 1 ≡ −2 ⇒ p = 3.
Thus no prime p > 5 arises here.

